0A Tough dull pinkish grey to blue-grey species , developing on dead attached twigs or branches. Spores elongated , allantoid to cylindric. The genus Peniophora. Not easy. Only the three commonest species are keyed here:02112143803
0B Softer white to cream species , on stumps or fallen wood. Spores narrow ellipsoid.02113143905
0Cystidia ± ellipsoid. Much-branched hyphae ['dendrohyphidia'] at margins of hymenium; spores 9-13 3.5-5 µm. 03121415400Peniophora lycii (Phillips 240)0
0Cystidia elongated , the encrusted portion conical. Dendrohyphidia absent.031215154104
0a Tightly attached; spores short , 7.5-9 3-3.5 µm 04151616420Peniophora cinerea (156)0
0b Rolling back from bark when mature , showing a dark underside; spores 9-13 x 3-4 µm. On oak and beech 04151716430Peniophora quercina (153) [Some species have gloeocystidia as well as or instead of metuloids] [If on conifers and spores amyloid see Amylostereum (195-197)]0
0Spores under 7 µm long , clamps absent (but texture very dense , so this is not always easy to verify)051318174406
0Spores larger , 7-10 3.5-5 µm , clamps present051319174508
0a Spores 3.5-4 1.5-2 µm , surface waxy with pimples (distinctive and soon recognisable in the field with a hand lens) 06182018460Scopuloides rimosa (166)0
0b Spores 5-7 2.5-3 µm 061821184707
0Covering conifer stumps. Texture very dense throughout , hyphae strongly agglutinated 07212219480Phlebiopsis gigantea (165)0
0Normally on deciduous wood. With a subiculum of broader more separable hyphae 07212319490Phanerochaete velutina (164)0
0a Cystidia cylindric , septate , with clamps at the septa just about visible through the encrustations 08192420500Hyphoderma setigerum (129)0
0b Cystidia tapered , abundant , non-septate 08192520510Hyphoderma puberum (127) [Most other Hyphoderma species have unencrusted cystidia]0
00-2026105400
0A Spores long cylindric , 12-15 x 2.5-3 µm. Cystidia narrow , encrusted , sharply pointed , abundant. Thin greyish patches on very rotten deciduous logs , esp. elm. 0922721560Subulicystidium longisporum (206)0
0B Spores globose to subglobose092282157010
0C Spores allantoid092292158014
0D Spores otherwise (ellipsoid , cylindric , etc.)092302159016
0Spores ornamented01028312260011
0Spores smooth , cystidia absent01028322261012
0a With clamps; with cylindric cystidia; spores warted 6-8 x 5-7 µm [Other similar species vary in spore size] 011313323620Hypochnicium punctulatum s.l.(135)0
0b No clamps or cystidia; spores spiny 5-6.5 µm ; fruit-body soft , yellowish. On very rotten wood 011313423630Tomentellopsis echinospora (252)0
0a Spores 8-10 x 6-8 µm , Hygrophanous , drying pale and smooth , but grey , opalescent , waxy , tuberculate when fresh. Abundant on fallen deciduous branches , esp. Fagus (in B&K as Cerocorticium c.) 012323524640Radulomyces confluens (93)0
0b Spores 4-5 x 3-4 µm , somewhat irregular. Hymenium cream tinged greenish , smooth with isolated warts. Basidia and hyphal segments notably short 012323624650Brevicellicium olivascens (116)0
0Spores small 4-5 x 2-2.5 µm. Hymenium verrucose , full of crystals and difficult to make out until vigorously squashed , then revealing 6-8 spored basidia with swollen bases ('urniform') typical of the genus. On wood or often growing over old polypores. Commonest member of a large genus 014293726660Sistotrema brinkmannii (188)0
0Spores very large , 15-19 x 5.5-6 µm. Recognisable in the field as a thin greasy film pushing back the bark of dead attached twigs 014293826670Vuilleminia comedens (217) [Some heteros , eg Eichleriella deglubens (17) , have similar spores]0
0Spores pale brown , 9-13 x 6-8 µm 01630392868017
0Spores hyaline , smaller01630402869018
0a Thin , spores dextrinoid 017394129700Coniophora arida (236)420
0[Membranomyces spurius sensu B&K (137) appears also to be merely young material of this species.]017394229710N0
0b Relatively thick , spores scarcely dextrinoid 017394329730Coniophora puteana (238)0
0a A high proportion of the hymenium made up of enclosed gloeocystidia (showing up most clearly in Melzer's)01840443074019
0b Gloeocystidia absent01840453075021
0** Spores inamyloid , 8-10 x 3.5-4.5 µm , ± cylindric. Gloeocystidia normally under 100 µm with pointed apex. Other exserted cystidia with encrusted swollen heads usually also present; a third type of cystidia (± globose 'stephanocysts') seldom seen except in v.young material. Rather variable but very common everywhere019444631760Hyphoderma praetermissum (125)0
0* Spores amyloid , cystidia to 100 µm long or more (often forming extensive growths over old decid. stumps etc.)01944473179020
0Spores cylindric , 4.5-5.5 x 2.5-3 µm , finely warted , strongly amyloid 020474832800Gloeocystidiellum porosum (105)0
0Spores ellipsoid , 6-8 x 4-5 µm , smooth. Luxuriant growths will emit a watery/milky juice when bruised (in B&K as Megalocystidium 1.) 020474932810Gloiothele lactescens (107)0
0Spores smooth 021455033830see continuation as Key 30
0Spores small and spiny02145513384022
0Spores 4.5-5.5 µm long , basidia pleural (ie developing laterally on their hyphae). Usually recognisable in the field from its fans of lemon yellow sterile hyphae , but these may be absent and the hymenium is pale brown. (in B&K as Trechispora v. , in NM3 as Ph. sulphurea) 022515234850Phlebiella vaga (117)0
0Spores under 5 µm long; basal hyphae often strongly swollen at the septa. Often with a finely hydnoid hymenium 022515334860Trechispora farinacea(112) [Several less common species have a smooth hymenium]0
00-305498900
0A Spores under 5 µm long0233553591024
0B Spores larger0233563592028
0On deciduous wood; cystidia absent02455573693025
0Usually on coniferous wood; cystidia numerous , narrow , projecting , usually septate with clamps , often ± capitate02455583694026
0a Spores 3.5-4 x 3-3.5 µm , ± thick-walled. Basidia with granules which stain in cotton blue. Hyphal strands running through the hymenium 025575937950Cristinia helvetica (89)0
0b Spores 3-5 x 2.5-4 µm. Basidia unremarkable , (B&K photo is upside down) [A similar species , T. confinis , is distinguished in NM3] 025576037960Trechispora cohaerens s.l. (111)0
0a With also plentiful 'lagenocystidia' (small encrusted needles) (in B&K as Grandinia.) 026586138970Hyphodontia alutaria (76)0
0b Very similar but ± lacking lagenocystidia 026586238980Hyphodontia pallidula (84)0
0Thin cobwebby species , easily detachable from the substrate (wood or leaf-litter)02856634099029
0Denser and usually thicker , tightly attached species028566440100031
0a Hyphae broad , 6-9 µm wide , tending to branch at right-angles , very distinctive; basidia 6- 8-spored , soon collapsing029636541101030
0b Hyphae narrower , 3-6 µm wide , basidia 2- 4-spored , clamps largely absent , but occasional on basal hyphae029636641102042
0Clamps absent; several further common Botryobasidium spp. , some only distinguishable by their anamorph states , e.g. 0306567421040Botryobasidium aureum - see Fig 1.0
0Clamps at all septa 0306568421050Botryobasidium subcoronatum (67)0
0b Hymenium smooth when young , becoming wrinkled. Clamps absent , spores 5-6 x 2.5 -3.5 µm On deciduous twigs and branches (in B&K as Meruliopsis c.) - see Fig 2. 0316469431060Byssomerulius corium (144)0
0a Hymenium verrucose to shortly spiny031647043107032
0c Hymenium smooth (or sometimes fissured when old)031647143108033
0Cystidia otherwise or absent. Hyphae ± thick-walled with prominent clamps. Crystals often present in hymenium (in B&K as Grandinia) 0327072441090Hyphodontia spp.(76-86) [Large genus with several fairly common species not always easy to distinguish. See key in NM3 and also Key 4 below.]90
0Cystidia with 'haloes' and other cystidia bearing stellate crystals , spores 6-7.5 ? 2.5-3.5 µm on conifer logs 0327073441100Resinicium bicolor (178)0
0Spores over 8 µm long; clamps present033717445111034
0Spores under 8 µm long033717545112035
0Spores 8-12 x 5-6 µm , pip-shaped; cystidia absent. Spores often adhering in fours in slide mounts. Usually developing narrow pilei , but sometimes fully resupinate. Common on recently fallen deciduous logs , esp. sawn ends. Characteristic cracks develop in hymenium with age 0347476461130Cylindrobasidium evolvens (92)0
0Spores ellipsoid to cylindric; cystidia present. Basidia and spores with oil drops in protoplasm [Not easy , several species key here.] 0347477461140Hyphoderma species0
0Clamps absent; spores 5-7 x 2.5-3.5 µm , cystidia narrow and tapered. Basal hyphae thick-walled , forming a distinctive loose mesh branching ± at rightangles 0357578471150Phanerochaete sordida (162) [Large genus , all species clampless , several not uncommon]0
0Clamps present; spores 5-6 x 3.5-4 µm , broadly ellipsoid , often with large oil drop. Fruit-body thin , chalk-white , esp. on Sambucus but also on many other deciduous hosts (in B&K as Lyomyces s.) 0357579471160Hyphodontia sambuci (139) [Small , protruding , capitate cystidia provide confirmation , but are not always plentiful]0
02-spored , spores 8-10 x 4-5 µm 0426680541170Athelia arachnoidea (49)0
04-spored , spores 6-8 x 2.5-3.5 µm 0426681541180Athelia epiphylla s.l. (52)0
